@database "mcp_gateway"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: mcp_gateway.guide 1.0 (27.10.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "MCP gateway"
@{h1}MCP gateway

An outside CLI -- Claude Code, Antigravity -- reaches tools of this
installation through this plugin, over MCP. It is meant for a caller that
starts such a CLI for a task (a coding run on a subscription instead of the
API) and wants it to work with the same tools an agent here would use.

Nothing is open by configuration alone. The caller issues a token for one
run, names the tools that run may call and what it may do with them, hands
the token to the CLI, and revokes it when the run ends. Every call goes
through one agent, the runner, whose allowlist is the ceiling of every token
and whose hooks see every call.

@{code}
For developers
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Grants: issue and revoke  " link grants}
  @{"  What a call goes through  " link calls}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Known gaps  " link gaps}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Two entries ship in @{tt}agents/mcp_gateway.yaml@{utt}, both switched off:

@{table}
Entry | Type | Is
mcp_gateway | mcp_gateway | the endpoint
mcp_gateway_runner | basic_agent | the agent every call runs through; nobody chats with it
@{body}

The runner carries @{tt}min_role: admin@{utt}: started directly as an agent, its
model could call every tool of its allowlist without a grant's pinned and
allowed values. The gateway's own calls do not pass this gate.

Enable both and give the runner the tools any run may ever call, with
@{tt}+@{utt} entries, in your own config:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    mcp_gateway:
      enabled: true
    mcp_gateway_runner:
      enabled: true
      agent_config:
        tools:
          allowed: ["+my_store/*"]
          blocked: ["my_store/my_store_purge"]
@{body}

@{h2}Settings of the gateway

@{table}
Setting | Default | Meaning
runner_agent | mcp_gateway_runner | the agent the calls go through
max_result_chars | 60000 | a result longer than this is refused, not cut: it would flood the CLI's context
@{body}

New entries take effect after a restart of the API.

@{h2}The route

The endpoint is @{tt}/plugins/<instance>/mcp@{utt}, by default
@{tt}http://127.0.0.1:<port>/plugins/mcp_gateway/mcp@{utt} -- the port the API
listens on (@{tt}PORT@{utt}, else @{tt}network.port@{utt}). A CLI cannot log in,
so @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt} opens this one route in both auth layers
(@{tt}endpoint_security@{utt} and @{tt}plugin_security@{utt}); the route itself
answers 401 to any request without the bearer token of a live grant, before
a byte reaches the MCP server. An instance of another name needs the same
two rules for its path.

It speaks MCP's Streamable HTTP through the mcp SDK's own server --
stateless, answers as JSON, every protocol revision the SDK speaks. There
is no server-sent event stream and no session to resume: each request
stands alone.
@endnode

@node grants "Grants: issue and revoke"
@{h1}Grants: issue and revoke

A grant lives in the memory of the API process and is found by the sha256 of
its token; the token itself is kept nowhere. Only code in that process can
issue one -- a worker process cannot hand a grant to the gateway.

@{code python}
from plugins.mcp_gateway.grants import issue, revoke

token = issue("ada", ["my_store_read", "my_store_search_*"],
              request_id="run-4711",
              pinned={"my_store/*": {"project_id": 12}},
              allowed={"my_store_read": {"operations[].op": ["get", "list"]}},
              session_id="sess-1", ttl_s=1800)
# hand the CLI: Authorization: Bearer <token>
revoke(token)
@{body}

@{h2}Arguments

@{table}
Argument | Meaning
user_id | the account the calls are made for; tools get it as @{tt}_user_id@{utt}
tools | fnmatch patterns on the flat tool name (with the instance prefix); a subset of the runner's allowlist, never more
request_id | prefix of every call's request id: @{tt}<request_id>_mcp001@{utt}, @{tt}_mcp002@{utt} ... -- costs of tools that call an LLM themselves are found by it
pinned | the run's tool bindings, @{tt}{tool pattern: {parameter: value}}@{utt}: set in every call of a tool the pattern matches; a call with another value is refused
allowed | @{tt}{tool pattern: {path: [values]}}@{utt}: for the tools the pattern matches, every value at the path must be one of these
session_id | the session the calls run in (@{tt}_session_id@{utt}); without it the calls have none
ttl_s | seconds the token holds (default 3600)
gateway | the instance (default @{tt}mcp_gateway@{utt})
@{body}

@{h2}Pinned parameters

@{tt}pinned@{utt} are the run's tool bindings, the same form an agent run
takes (@{tt}Agent.run_events(tool_bindings=...)@{utt}): each pattern -- the
allowlist's syntax, @{tt}server/tool@{utt}, @{tt}server/*@{utt}, or a
wildcard on the full path or the bare name; a bare name without @{tt}*@{utt}
names a server, as in the allowlist -- holds the tools it matches to its
values. A tool no pattern matches is not touched.

A bound parameter is the run's, not the model's: it is left out of the tool
list the CLI sees, set in every call, and a call that names it with another
value is refused -- the model learns it asked for the wrong thing instead of
being corrected without a word. A tool a pattern matches that does not take
the parameter, or that two patterns hold to different values, is not shown
and cannot be called with this token: it could not be held to the run's
values. The values go in after the runner's pre_tool_call hooks, in
@{tt}dispatch_tool_call@{utt} -- the same check an agent's own tool loop
holds its run's bindings with.

Values are text, numbers or true/false. Names beginning with @{tt}_@{utt}
and the request id are refused: runtime parameters are the framework's (pass
@{tt}session_id@{utt} for that one).

@{h2}Allowed values

A path names keys joined by @{tt}.@{utt}; @{tt}[]@{utt} after a key means
every element of that list: @{tt}operations[].op@{utt} is the @{tt}op@{utt} of
every operation. A call where the path is missing is refused as well --
the tool's own default could be a value the rule keeps out. An empty list
passes: there is nothing in it. This is how a token reads but does not
write a tool that does both through one parameter.

@{h2}Errors

@{tt}issue@{utt} raises @{tt}ValueError@{utt} for an argument that cannot
hold (empty account, tools or request id; a lifetime of 0 or less; a
pinned runtime parameter; a path that is none, an empty list of values) and
@{tt}LookupError@{utt} when the gateway does not serve in this process (not
enabled, or a process whose app does not mount its route: agent-cli, a
worker -- a CLI handed a token there would reach no gateway that knows it).
@{tt}revoke@{utt} returns False when there was nothing to revoke.
A revoked grant stops a call of it that is still running -- the tool's task
is cancelled, the hooks see the cancellation token -- and its token answers
401 from then on. A grant whose time runs out stops its calls the same way,
and so does stopping the plugin, which ends all its grants. @{tt}revoke@{utt}
may be called from any thread.
@endnode

@node calls "What a call goes through"
@{h1}What a call goes through

@{bullet}The route: no live grant for the bearer token, 401.
@{bullet}tools/list and tools/call ask the same question -- which tools this
grant may call: the runner's own tool list (its allowlist and blocklist,
every tool interface, exactly what the runner would send a model), narrowed
to the grant's patterns, without tools its pinned values cannot hold,
without external MCP tools. What is not listed does not exist
for the client.
@{bullet}A call: pinned values checked, allowed values checked,
the arguments checked against the tool's parameter schema (unknown
parameters, missing required ones, types).
@{bullet}Then @{tt}dispatch_tool_call@{utt} of the runner with
@{tt}hook_source="mcp_gateway"@{utt}: its @{tt}pre_tool_call@{utt} hooks may
change or block the call, the tool runs with the grant's account, session
and request id (also as the run's context: what the tool reports without
an agent at hand is the run's) and reports its status lines as for any
agent, the @{tt}post_tool_call@{utt} hooks see the result. A grant revoked or
expired meanwhile stops the call.
@{bullet}The result goes back as JSON text; one that reports an error
(@{tt}status: error@{utt}, or an @{tt}error@{utt} without a status) is
flagged as an error, one over @{tt}max_result_chars@{utt} is refused.
@{bullet}One log line per call, a failed one too: gateway, grant key (the first 12 characters
of the token's hash -- never the token), tool, ok or error, duration.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool list

Each tool the grant may call, with its own name and description, and its
parameter schema without the pinned parameters. Nothing of the gateway
itself is added.

@{h2}Error answers, verbatim

@{table}
When | The answer
a tool outside the grant | Unknown tool 'NAME': call one from tools/list.
another value for a pinned parameter | KEY is fixed to VALUE for this run; this call asked for OTHER. Leave KEY out.
a value outside the allowed ones | PATH must be one of [VALUES] for this run, not 'VALUE'
an allowed path left out | PATH is required for this run, one of [VALUES]
a parameter the tool does not have | NAME: unknown parameter(s) [KEYS]. Valid: [PARAMETERS]
a schema violation | NAME: WHERE: what the validator says
a result too long | NAME: the result has N characters, more than the M this gateway hands on. Ask for less (fewer ids, a line range, a narrower query).
a hook blocked the call | the hook's own reason
the grant ended during the call | NAME: the run's token was revoked or expired; the call was stopped.
the runner is missing | The gateway's runner agent 'NAME' is not available.
@{body}

@{h2}Token and cache effect

None on this installation's agents: the gateway adds nothing to any prompt
or history. What the CLI keeps of the answers is the CLI's.
@endnode

@node gaps "Known gaps"
@{h1}Known gaps

@{bullet}One process. Grants live in the API process's memory; a caller in
another process cannot issue one the gateway knows (it is refused there),
and a restart ends every grant.
@{bullet}One instance. @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt} opens the route of
@{tt}mcp_gateway@{utt} only; a second instance answers every request with
401 until its route is opened there as well.
@{bullet}Pinned and allowed values hold for what a tool takes as a
parameter. A tool that finds its target by another id (an item id that
belongs to some project) is only as narrow as that tool's own check of
the id against the pinned parameter.
@{bullet}No streaming: a long tool call answers when it is done. The CLI's
own MCP timeout applies.
@{bullet}Tools of external MCP servers are not passed on: the runner cannot
dispatch them programmatically.
@endnode
